1
00:00:19,689 --> 00:00:23,473
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is the first ever sutta that the Buddha gave.

2
00:00:24,614 --> 00:00:27,978
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the setting in motion of the wheel of the dhamma.

3
00:00:29,497 --> 00:00:30,298
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus have I heard.

4
00:00:30,658 --> 00:00:36,121
[SPEAKER_00]: On one occasion, the Blessed One was dwelling at Baranasi in the Deer Park at Isipatana.

5
00:00:37,002 --> 00:00:40,044
[SPEAKER_00]: There, the Blessed One addressed the bhikkhus of the Group of Five thus.

6
00:00:41,045 --> 00:00:46,849
[SPEAKER_00]: Bhikkhus, these two sides should not be followed by one who has gone forth into homelessness.

7
00:00:47,469 --> 00:00:47,909
[SPEAKER_00]: What to?

8
00:00:48,770 --> 00:00:51,732
[SPEAKER_00]: The pursuit of sensual happiness and sensual pleasures

9
00:00:52,192 --> 00:01:04,515
[SPEAKER_00]: which is low, vulgar, the way of worklings, innoble, unbeneficial, and the pursuit of self-mortification, which is painful, innoble, unbeneficial.

10
00:01:05,696 --> 00:01:20,900
[SPEAKER_00]: Without veering towards either of these sides, the Tathāgata has awakened to the middle way, which gives rise to wisdom, which gives rise to knowledge, which leads to peace, to direct knowledge, to enlightenment, to nibbāna.

11
00:01:24,072 --> 00:01:24,272
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

12
00:01:57,423 --> 00:02:09,610
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the difference between the middle way that the Buddha discovered and asceticism and indulgence?

13
00:02:12,155 --> 00:02:14,676
[SPEAKER_00]: Back in the day, some people accused the Buddha of both.

14
00:02:15,057 --> 00:02:25,782
[SPEAKER_00]: They would accuse him of being an ascetic, of being a denialist, and some others would accuse him of being indulgent.

15
00:02:26,643 --> 00:02:32,846
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is it that really distinguishes the middle way from those two sides?

16
00:02:50,667 --> 00:02:53,469
[SPEAKER_02]: Seeing body as not-self.

17
00:02:53,489 --> 00:02:53,769
[SPEAKER_02]: See?

18
00:02:54,110 --> 00:03:09,441
[SPEAKER_02]: Seeing body as not-self, like indulging in sensual pleasures, obviously takes body... has body as a central reference point, and the self-mortification as well.

19
00:03:10,782 --> 00:03:18,969
[SPEAKER_02]: So, seeing that that body is not-self, seems to me, is one part of discerning the middle way.

20
00:03:20,779 --> 00:03:25,862
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, but that would be kind of farther down the development.

21
00:03:25,902 --> 00:03:32,207
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, let's say, somebody is starting out and the Buddha tells them...

22
00:03:33,587 --> 00:03:38,271
[SPEAKER_00]: So, okay, how do you... You know the classic, classic formula about somebody here in the dhamma?

23
00:03:38,831 --> 00:03:49,078
[SPEAKER_00]: It says that they go forth and then they take on these precepts, which is basically the eight precepts, including saliva-si,

24
00:03:50,179 --> 00:03:56,866
[SPEAKER_00]: sense of restraint, moderation in eating, seclusion...

25
00:03:59,756 --> 00:04:03,138
[SPEAKER_00]: Many people would say that those things are a form of asceticism.

26
00:04:04,179 --> 00:04:14,805
[SPEAKER_00]: Like not, yeah, not eating in the afternoon, not delighting in company, living away from others.

27
00:04:15,526 --> 00:04:20,168
[SPEAKER_00]: They would say that that is an extreme path of asceticism.

28
00:04:21,249 --> 00:04:25,852
[SPEAKER_00]: And then other people who kind of go further into

29
00:04:26,710 --> 00:04:36,072
[SPEAKER_00]: People who are proper ascetics, they would say, well, the fact that you eat in the morning, you eat only once, but you eat anything, kind of.

30
00:04:36,652 --> 00:04:53,035
[SPEAKER_00]: You eat, like, delicious food sometimes, and... or they would say, well, when it's cold, you use heating, and when it's hot, you use fans or a conditioner.

31
00:04:53,435 --> 00:04:54,155
[SPEAKER_00]: Therefore, you're not

32
00:04:55,301 --> 00:05:01,284
[SPEAKER_00]: The ascetics would accuse people who are practicing the Middle Way from being indulgent.

33
00:05:02,005 --> 00:05:10,529
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why is none of those criticisms valid?

34
00:05:10,569 --> 00:05:13,311
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is the Middle Way generally above those two?

35
00:05:13,371 --> 00:05:15,872
[SPEAKER_00]: What is it that distinguishes them?

36
00:05:18,607 --> 00:05:42,212
[SPEAKER_00]: Without, like, just from that, from a beginner's point of view, not necessarily, like, later on, because it's saying there, like, the the the Noble Eightfold Path, like, knowing the body is not self, and and all that stuff, that would kind of, um... You don't start with that, let's put it that way.

37
00:05:42,272 --> 00:05:42,632
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, I see.

38
00:05:42,692 --> 00:05:42,932
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

39
00:05:43,552 --> 00:05:43,832
[SPEAKER_01]: Mm-hmm.

40
00:05:45,692 --> 00:05:47,793
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, to some degree, you could, um,

41
00:05:54,676 --> 00:05:55,597
[SPEAKER_01]: A beginner's point.

42
00:05:56,397 --> 00:06:04,043
[SPEAKER_01]: You might recognize, to some degree, the unsatisfactory nature of indulging in pleasures.

43
00:06:06,384 --> 00:06:17,993
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess for someone who has, what do you call it, practiced more strict asceticism, they might be able to recognize that that's not

44
00:06:19,775 --> 00:06:20,075
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

45
00:06:20,235 --> 00:06:22,837
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't bring satis... well, satisfaction.

46
00:06:23,257 --> 00:06:24,178
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't, um...

47
00:06:29,100 --> 00:06:29,601
[SPEAKER_00]: Very good.

48
00:06:33,337 --> 00:06:35,179
[SPEAKER_01]: It kind of feeds itself, so to say.

49
00:06:35,599 --> 00:06:37,761
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the more you do it, the more you need to do it.

50
00:06:38,341 --> 00:06:44,987
[SPEAKER_01]: Kind of become dependent on it, on that practice, whatever it is, for your peace, so to say.

51
00:06:45,668 --> 00:06:49,111
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether it's indulging in social pleasures or indulging in...

52
00:06:50,992 --> 00:06:52,573
[SPEAKER_00]: Self-mortification.

53
00:06:52,593 --> 00:06:58,715
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're saying, like, indulgence in self-mortification, well, it's also a form of indulgence.

54
00:06:58,755 --> 00:06:59,495
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

55
00:07:00,175 --> 00:07:13,840
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, how do you know whether... because, externally, it might look very similar, especially to someone who isn't, kind of, who doesn't have experience with the practice themselves.

56
00:07:14,620 --> 00:07:15,961
[SPEAKER_00]: One person who is practicing

57
00:07:17,401 --> 00:07:26,006
[SPEAKER_00]: Indulgence in self-mortification and the person who is just practicing renunciation, which you could say is the middle way.

58
00:07:26,947 --> 00:07:38,073
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, what would be the distinguishing aspect that they can know for themselves, not necessarily by what others are judging?

59
00:07:40,374 --> 00:07:44,797
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the kind of state of mind or the intention behind doing it.

60
00:07:46,833 --> 00:07:50,715
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what would be the intention behind someone who's practicing self-mortification?

61
00:07:50,735 --> 00:07:58,558
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, how is it... Well, yeah, essentially, it's a little bit how you feel, basically.

62
00:07:58,578 --> 00:08:07,582
[SPEAKER_01]: So, trying to... trying to change the feeling in such a way that pleases you, basically.

63
00:08:07,982 --> 00:08:10,923
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether it's pleasant or painful.

64
00:08:12,895 --> 00:08:21,198
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, wanting to get rid of things is an attitude that is behind the self-motivation, and wanting to get things is an attitude behind sensuality.

65
00:08:21,998 --> 00:08:23,098
[SPEAKER_02]: And they both miss the point.

66
00:08:24,478 --> 00:08:31,581
[SPEAKER_00]: And, in fact, the attitude behind, like, self-motivation, you're getting rid of things because you also want to get something.

67
00:08:31,921 --> 00:08:32,381
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

68
00:08:32,441 --> 00:08:39,483
[SPEAKER_00]: But not in the same course level as sexual pleasures, but you still want to get.

69
00:08:42,444 --> 00:08:48,068
[SPEAKER_00]: Your idea of what enlightenment is, you're chasing after it through this practice.

70
00:08:48,788 --> 00:09:05,418
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why one very strong sign of somebody who's practicing self-mortification is that if they're suddenly not able to do those practices that they're

71
00:09:06,551 --> 00:09:15,056
[SPEAKER_00]: Holding on to, as a means of supposedly freeing themselves from suffering, they will suffer.

72
00:09:15,636 --> 00:09:31,005
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, those practices, even if they don't depend on the world anymore, like, they don't depend on course, sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, their emotional dependence is on this practice now.

73
00:09:31,386 --> 00:09:31,666
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

74
00:09:31,866 --> 00:09:35,068
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, if they don't do it, they will suffer

75
00:09:36,582 --> 00:09:42,135
[SPEAKER_00]: It's pretty much the same as somebody who is independent on ten subjects.

76
00:09:42,175 --> 00:09:42,476
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

77
00:09:43,518 --> 00:09:46,185
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's just that...

78
00:09:47,830 --> 00:09:57,618
[SPEAKER_00]: Generally, it is harder to be forced to give up those practices than it is to lose sensual pleasures.

79
00:09:58,458 --> 00:10:10,247
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, you would suffer more, on average, if you are engaged with sensuality, than if you're practicing, like, a good level of self-modification.

80
00:10:10,808 --> 00:10:14,691
[SPEAKER_00]: You would actually suffer... your pleasure is more impermanent.

81
00:10:15,431 --> 00:10:29,376
[SPEAKER_00]: If it depends on sensual pleasures, but when it comes down to it, both of them are absolutely not ideal, because both of them are dependent on, yeah, on things that are not in your control.

82
00:10:29,716 --> 00:10:29,936
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

83
00:10:30,657 --> 00:10:35,338
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's that sutta that talks about the

84
00:10:38,374 --> 00:10:55,328
[SPEAKER_00]: It's called the bait, and it talks about four types of deer herd, and there's the first one who kind of just carelessly goes into the bait, gets, you know, gets trapped by the hunter, and then the hunter does with them whatever he wants.

85
00:10:56,008 --> 00:11:03,050
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, there's the other one who kind of recognizes, sees that the others are are falling into demise on account of this.

86
00:11:03,310 --> 00:11:06,551
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, they decide to completely shun the bait.

87
00:11:06,731 --> 00:11:07,771
[SPEAKER_00]: They go into the mountains.

88
00:11:08,191 --> 00:11:12,632
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh... for a while, they're free from the trap.

89
00:11:13,892 --> 00:11:18,894
[SPEAKER_00]: But, eventually, eh... there is no way that they can stay there forever.

90
00:11:19,974 --> 00:11:21,314
[SPEAKER_00]: So, something will have to happen.

91
00:11:21,594 --> 00:11:23,555
[SPEAKER_00]: Something will have to, eh...

92
00:11:25,267 --> 00:11:29,434
[SPEAKER_00]: prohibit their continual engagement in that practice.

93
00:11:29,995 --> 00:11:34,103
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they have to come back to where the debate was before.

94
00:11:34,744 --> 00:11:37,508
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, because they didn't learn how to

95
00:11:39,070 --> 00:11:44,453
[SPEAKER_00]: manoeuvre around the bait, all they know is the same old habit of biting into it again.

96
00:11:44,694 --> 00:11:46,135
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, they get caught.

97
00:11:46,295 --> 00:11:52,959
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, the third one is a kind of a cellular problem that gets caught in views.

98
00:11:53,299 --> 00:11:54,360
[SPEAKER_00]: We can discuss that later.

99
00:11:55,821 --> 00:11:57,162
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, only the fourth

100
00:11:58,122 --> 00:12:10,570
[SPEAKER_00]: A deer herd knows how to stay where the hunt... where the trap is, where the hunter... in the range where the hunter is setting his traps, but they have learned how to not bite them.

101
00:12:11,851 --> 00:12:24,719
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's... that's... that is like... in a person who is practicing the Middle Way, the difference is that they have recognized that the baits are not optional.

102
00:12:26,084 --> 00:12:33,168
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, experiencing pleasant sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, is not actually optional.

103
00:12:34,169 --> 00:12:51,339
[SPEAKER_00]: Whereas, a person who is practicing self-mortification is kind of having this continuous, perpetual quest, never-ending quest, to prevent themselves from experiencing agreeable things.

104
00:12:52,160 --> 00:12:53,761
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's impossible, because

105
00:12:55,197 --> 00:12:59,899
[SPEAKER_00]: When you do that long enough, your avoidance of agreeable things becomes agreeable.

106
00:13:01,159 --> 00:13:06,261
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's why it's like, if you... you can never succeed in it without a contradiction.

107
00:13:07,061 --> 00:13:09,462
[SPEAKER_00]: And the final point of that is like, you get to

108
00:13:10,870 --> 00:13:27,203
[SPEAKER_00]: Where the Buddha actually went, just before he kind of turned around and realized that that was the wrong path, it's like, I have to stop breathing, so as to truly not be dependent on any kind of pleasure, because anything less than that, if your perception of

109
00:13:28,444 --> 00:13:31,327
[SPEAKER_00]: of indulgence is just the pleasure existing.

110
00:13:31,927 --> 00:13:42,536
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, the only true way to kind of fulfill that ideal is to go to that extreme, like, okay, I won't even breathe now, because, you know, that is causing a form of pleasure.

111
00:13:43,256 --> 00:13:56,767
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why self-mortification, in the end, is futile, but it still has to, like, one has to give things up.

112
00:13:58,455 --> 00:14:10,617
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not that one just, oh, I stay where I am, and I just abstractly think about the middle way, and that's how I'm partaking the Buddha's instruction.

113
00:14:10,637 --> 00:14:13,238
[SPEAKER_00]: There are factually things that you have to give up.

114
00:14:14,598 --> 00:14:17,898
[SPEAKER_00]: Like I said before, if you're not keeping the precepts, you have to take them on.

115
00:14:17,939 --> 00:14:21,419
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to, if you're not practicing sense restraint, you have to take it on.

116
00:14:21,939 --> 00:14:25,260
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, how would a person go about

117
00:14:27,881 --> 00:14:34,024
[SPEAKER_00]: Changing their behavior, which, nowadays, is going to start from the default point of indulgence.

118
00:14:34,204 --> 00:14:35,425
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

119
00:14:35,605 --> 00:14:48,011
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how do they, kind of, go about taking on a closer version of the Middle Way?

120
00:14:48,031 --> 00:14:51,873
[SPEAKER_00]: Because another thing to note is that the Middle Way is not a matter of choice.

121
00:14:51,953 --> 00:14:55,315
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, often, it's thought that the Middle Way is just about

122
00:14:56,842 --> 00:15:00,924
[SPEAKER_00]: Choosing kind of a intermediate... let's say... let's say...

123
00:15:00,944 --> 00:15:02,845
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know... there's...

124
00:15:04,146 --> 00:15:15,511
[SPEAKER_00]: I have a choice between eating very delicious food and then very disagreeable food that tastes like paper or something, and I kind of choose something in between, and then that's the middle way.

125
00:15:16,532 --> 00:15:22,975
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, the middle... as the sutta I just read says, the middle way is the noble wayful path.

126
00:15:23,935 --> 00:15:27,497
[SPEAKER_00]: And it starts with the right view, meaning understanding the Four Noble Truths.

127
00:15:27,617 --> 00:15:34,799
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the Middle Way can only be practiced because the Middle Way is the Four Noble Truth, by the way.

128
00:15:34,819 --> 00:15:41,822
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like, only by knowing that truth, that this is the Middle Way, is the person able, would be able to follow it.

129
00:15:42,402 --> 00:15:48,305
[SPEAKER_00]: So, but how does a person kind of set themselves, set themselves up

130
00:15:50,005 --> 00:16:02,230
[SPEAKER_00]: through the things that they can do, like the virtue and sense restraint, so that they do it more in the direction of the middle way than than asceticism or self-mortification.

131
00:16:02,931 --> 00:16:18,057
[SPEAKER_00]: Why would they have to, um... What would they... As I said, since externally it can look the same, what is it that internally they would have to do so as to not fall into that trap?

132
00:16:25,706 --> 00:16:27,807
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, they need to know how to attend to things rightly.

133
00:16:28,887 --> 00:16:39,932
[SPEAKER_02]: And based on that right attention, they will be able to discern whether they're acting out of wholesome or unwholesome.

134
00:16:40,711 --> 00:16:43,593
[SPEAKER_02]: And then that distinction might still be somewhat blurred.

135
00:16:43,853 --> 00:16:46,936
[SPEAKER_02]: It will only be properly clear once the view is right.

136
00:16:47,676 --> 00:16:50,138
[SPEAKER_02]: But that's also not going to happen miraculously.

137
00:16:50,238 --> 00:16:52,640
[SPEAKER_02]: That is sort of... One needs to work on that.

138
00:16:52,760 --> 00:16:59,285
[SPEAKER_02]: But our one is attending to things, which is the yoniso, manasikāra.

139
00:16:59,405 --> 00:17:03,368
[SPEAKER_02]: That is going to be the factor there that needs to be worked on.

140
00:17:03,868 --> 00:17:08,971
[SPEAKER_02]: In order for the Noble Eightfold Path to become actual in one's experience.

141
00:17:09,211 --> 00:17:09,372
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

142
00:17:10,312 --> 00:17:25,962
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, that is... there is actually one sutra that talks about how the acquisition of yonison manasikāra is like the the the omen

143
00:17:28,900 --> 00:17:37,409
[SPEAKER_00]: So, once a person has understood yoniso manasikāra, that is basically the condition.

144
00:17:37,549 --> 00:17:48,001
[SPEAKER_00]: And there's another one that says yoniso manasikāra and the utterance of another, meaning, you know, somebody who has understood the dhamma,

145
00:17:48,741 --> 00:17:51,223
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, that's true.

146
00:17:51,243 --> 00:17:52,384
[SPEAKER_00]: It's yoniso manasikāra.

147
00:17:52,404 --> 00:17:54,566
[SPEAKER_00]: But how would you practice that concretely?

148
00:17:54,606 --> 00:18:00,350
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, like, for example, you might be... like, the actual middle way,

149
00:18:15,353 --> 00:18:19,756
[SPEAKER_00]: As I was saying before, it's not something that you can just fix down as a code.

150
00:18:20,277 --> 00:18:20,897
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

151
00:18:21,097 --> 00:18:28,522
[SPEAKER_00]: Because sometimes, today, doing one thing will be rooted in indulgence.

152
00:18:29,183 --> 00:18:34,066
[SPEAKER_00]: Tomorrow, doing the same thing might be rooted in self-mortification.

153
00:18:34,686 --> 00:18:35,247
[SPEAKER_00]: Based on

154
00:18:36,257 --> 00:18:42,563
[SPEAKER_00]: Something that a person has to learn how to discern, so as to start working on the middle way.

155
00:18:43,183 --> 00:18:44,545
[SPEAKER_02]: So... What is that thing?

156
00:18:44,585 --> 00:18:44,805
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

157
00:18:45,145 --> 00:18:46,446
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that is one's intentions.

158
00:18:46,827 --> 00:18:46,987
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

159
00:18:47,087 --> 00:18:48,028
[SPEAKER_02]: Behind one's actions.

160
00:18:48,528 --> 00:18:50,990
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, like, why am I doing this now?

161
00:18:51,651 --> 00:19:01,340
[SPEAKER_02]: Am I doing this because I want to avoid experiencing something else, or because I want to chase something which I, which I think I'm lacking right now, which is, which is already based on

162
00:19:02,322 --> 00:19:10,250
[SPEAKER_02]: On regarding things wrongly, this whole notion of something being missing, right here and now, and to chase it.

163
00:19:11,692 --> 00:19:13,874
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then, again, externally...

164
